---
import TopicPage from '../../../../components/TopicPage.astro';
import Widget from '../../../../components/Widget.astro';

const disinfectionCfg = {
  params: [
    { name: 'ct', label: 'CT value (C × t)', min: 1, max: 60, step: 1, value: 15, unit: 'mg·min/L' },
    { name: 'ph', label: 'pH', min: 6, max: 9, step: 0.1, value: 7.5, unit: '' },
  ],
  readouts: [
    { label: 'Giardia inactivation (Chick–Watson approx.)', expr: 'ct*(1/(12*(1+(ph-7)*0.6)))', unit: 'log' },
  ],
};

const chemistryCfg = {
  steps: [
    { math: '1 · Equilibrium sets the chemistry', note: 'Carbonate buffering, solubility and pH govern what dissolves, precipitates and scales — the chemistry of every pipe and river.' },
    { math: '2 · Kinetics sets the clock', note: 'How fast pollutants decay or reactions complete is kinetics — the difference between a treatment that works and one that is still working tomorrow.' },
    { math: '3 · Microbes are the workforce', note: 'Activated sludge, nitrification, anaerobic digestion: modern wastewater treatment is deliberately cultivated microbial ecology.' },
    { math: '4 · Pathogens set the bar', note: 'Disinfection targets organisms by log-reduction — 99.9% (3-log) or 99.99% (4-log) removal depending on the pathogen\'s risk.' },
    { math: '5 · CT ties it together', note: 'Disinfection strength is concentration × contact time (CT), adjusted for temperature and pH — the design equation of every chlorinated system.' },
  ],
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/environmental-engineering/foundational/water-chemistry-and-microbiology">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Environmental engineering's water work rests on two sciences: chemistry (what is dissolved,
    and what will react) and microbiology (who is living in it, and what they eat). Every
    treatment plant is an arrangement of tanks that exploit exactly these two: chemistry to
    remove chemicals, biology to remove biodegradables, and disinfection to finish the pathogens.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="equilibrium, kinetics, bugs and the CT rule" widget="equationStep" config={chemistryCfg} />

  <h2>Disinfection as a design equation</h2>
  <p>
    Chlorine does not kill by presence alone — it kills by concentration held for time. Regulators
    encode this as the CT value, corrected for temperature and pH (free chlorine weakens as pH
    rises, because it shifts to the less potent hypochlorite ion form). Move the sliders: the same
    chlorine budget buys less inactivation at high pH. That is real design arithmetic.
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="CT-based inactivation under pH and dose" widget="sliderLab" config={disinfectionCfg} />

  <h2>The microbial workforce</h2>
  <p>
    Wastewater treatment is farming: operators grow specific microbial communities that consume
    organic matter (activated sludge), convert ammonia to nitrate (nitrification) and, in the
    anaerobic digesters, make biogas. The engineer's job is to keep the farm's environment right —
    oxygen, food, solids age — because when the ecology crashes, the effluent fails.
  </p>

  <h2>Why the foundation matters</h2>
  <p>
    Every later topic — coagulation chemistry, membrane fouling, nutrient removal, lead solubility
    in drinking water pipes — is an application of this unit's equilibrium, kinetic and microbial
    logic. Flint's drinking-water crisis was, at root, a corrosion chemistry failure. The science
    is the safety.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> NCEES FE Environmental specifications; Benjamin &amp; Lawler, <em>Water Quality Engineering</em>; Bitton, <em>Wastewater Microbiology</em>.</p>
</TopicPage>
